After the [[partition of the Ottoman Empire]], British-ruled [[Mandatory Palestine]] began in 1922.{{sfnm |Sayigh |2023 |1p=281 |Manna |2013 |2p=89 |Masalha |2012 |3p=33, 54, and 150 |Wolfe |2012 |4p=143 |5a1=Davis |5y=2011 |5p=6 |6a1=Morris |6y=2008 |6pp=9-14 |7a1=Sa'di |7y=2007 |7pp=288-290}} By then, Jews had become about 10% of the population.{{sfnm 
|Sabbagh-Khoury |2023 |1p=53 |1ps=, "around 12 percent [Jews]"
|Pappe |2022 |2p=79 |2ps=, "They [Palestinians] represented 90 per cent of the inhabitants, but were treated as if they constituted only 50 per cent"
|Davis |2011 |3p=6 |3ps=, "11 percent [Jews]"}} Both the Balfour Declaration and the [[Mandate for Palestine]] called the 90% Arab population "existing non-Jewish communities".<ref>{{harvnb |Sayigh |2023 |p=281 |ps=, "A more dangerous discursive deformation was the Balfour Declaration's designation of the Palestinians as 'existing non-Jewish communities' contrasted with 'the Jewish people' [Cronin 2017]. The political implications of this distinction are evident: a 'people' was qualified for nation/statehood, whereas disparate 'communities' were not."}};
{{harvnb |Khalidi |2020 |p=27 |ps=, "Significantly, the overwhelming Arab majority of the population (around 94 percent at that time) went unmentioned by Balfour, except in a backhanded way as the 'existing non-Jewish communities in Palestine.{{'"}}}};
{{harvnb |Slater |2020 |p=39 |ps=, "... the Balfour Doctrine and the League Mandate were conditional, stipulating that the 'non-Jewish' communities of Palestine{{snd}}some 90 percent of the indigenous peoples!{{snd}}must retain their 'civil and religious rights.{{'"}}}};
{{harvnb |Wolfe |2012 |p=146 |ps=, "The Mandate's preamble included a safeguard clause protecting the rights of 'existing non-Jewish communities'. This clause is significant on a number of counts, not least the transience implied in the term 'existing', whose suggestion of temporariness was reinforced by the designation of 91 per cent of the population as 'non-Jewish'."}};
{{harvnb |Shlaim |2009 |p=23 |ps=, "On the other hand, to refer to 90 per cent of the population as 'the non-Jewish communities in Palestine' was arrogant, dismissive and even racist. It was also the worst kind of imperial double standard, implying that there was one law for the Jews, and one law for everybody else."}};
{{harvnb |Morris |2008 |pp=9–10}}</ref>

In February 1947, after [[World War II]] and [[the Holocaust]], the British declared they would end the Mandate and submit Palestine's future to the newly created [[United Nations]] for resolution.<ref>{{harvnb |Pappe |2022 |pp=111–113}};
