=== Nov 1947 – May 1948 ===
[[1947–1948 civil war in Mandatory Palestine|Small-scale local skirmishes]] began on 30 November and gradually escalated until March 1948.<ref>{{harvnb |Manna |2022 |p=293 n. 18 |ps=, "Some researchers, particularly on the Israeli side, describe the events of the war in its first months as a "civil war." This description is inaccurate and controversial; it is preferable to divide the war into two stages without describing the first stage as a civil war."}};
{{harvnb |Pappe |2022 |pp=118–120 |ps=, "[p. 118] The next day brought the first outburst of intra-communal violence, activated by hot-headed youth on both sides ... A slow deterioration into a widespread civil war in the next few months generated second thoughts in the UN, and in Washington, about the desirability, indeed, the feasibility, of the partition plan. But it was too late for a large number of Palestinians, evicted from their houses after their leaders lost the early battles with the Jewish forces ... [p. 120] Until March 1948, clashes between the two communities, beginning the day after the UN partition plan was accepted by the General Assembly, were scattered, random and uncontrolled."}};
{{harvnb |Khalidi |2020 |p=73 |ps=, "Like a slow, seemingly endless train wreck, the Nakba unfolded over a period of many months. Its first stage, from November 30, 1947, until the final withdrawal of British forces and the establishment of Israel on May 15, 1948, witnessed successive defeats by Zionist paramilitary groups, including the Haganah and the Irgun, of the poorly armed and organized Palestinians and the Arab volunteers who had come to help them."}};
{{harvnb |Cohen |2017 |p=78 |ps=, "The outbreak of hostilities immediately after the UN vote in favor of the Partition Plan ..."}};
{{harvnb |Davis |2011 |p=235 n. 1 |ps=, "The fighting began in late 1947, following the United Nations decision to partition Palestine into a Jewish state and an Arab state, and continued until Israel reached separate truce agreements with Egypt, Lebanon, Jordan, and Syria in Rhodes between February and July of 1949 (Shlaim 2000, pp. 41–47)."}};
{{harvnb |Morris |2008 |pp=75–78 |ps=, "[p. 77] This stage was characterized by gradually expanding, continuous, small-scale, small-unit fighting. There was terrorism, and counterterrorist strikes, in the towns and ambushes along the roads. Arab armed bands attacked Jewish settlements, and Haganah units occasionally retaliated. It was formless{{snd}}there were no front lines (except along the seams between the two communities in the main, mixed towns), no armies moving back and forth, no pitched battles, and no conquests of territory."}};
